// expect: refuse E_EffectMismatch
// A DECLARED ROW REFUTED BY THE BODY IS DIAGNOSED AND STILL EMITTED. `bad()`
// declares !E. Its LEFT operand performs E under a ~> h that absorbs it —
// sound. Its RIGHT operand performs E through a second, independent
// higher-order call that no install covers, so the declared !E is a false
// absence proof. The medium SAYS so — effect row mismatch: !E + Any vs E —
// and then writes 7,696 bytes of WAT and exits 0.
//
// MEASURED 2026-09-22 through the pinned boot and through the rows-as-cells
// wheel alike, both identical: the diagnostic fires, the executable does not
// refuse. Its control, mn-sibling-mask-control, is the same body with the
// install removed; there the class that fires is E_EffectUnhandled, which IS
// armed, and the compile refuses with zero bytes. So one install is the whole
// difference between a proof the medium enforces and a proof it merely
// mentions.
//
// The pair was written to test a different question — whether a mask on one
// operand of a fold silences its sibling — and answered it: it does not. Both
// compilers charge the sibling's E to bad() and report the mismatch, which is
// the row reading being right. What the pair found instead is the arming gap:
// re-measured 2026-09-23, the emitted executable faults out of bounds in
// ev_declaring_node, the uncovered op reading evidence no install threaded.
// E_EffectMismatch was armed in the landing that banked this fixture, and the
// contract it holds is the refusal.
effect E { op() -> Int }

handler h {
  op() => resume(1),
}

fn run(f) = f()

fn run2(g) = g()

fn bad() with !E = ((run(() => op())) ~> h) + run2(() => op())

fn main() = bad()
